-- #1110 PR C corpus — the ENTRY, importing BOTH `Speak`s.
--
-- Two same-named interfaces from two unrelated modules are in this module's scope
-- at once.  Every `Speak` written below therefore has two candidate declarations
-- and exactly one spelling, so the origin the occurrence acquires is decided by
-- resolve's precedence rule (own > imports > prelude; within imports, later wins —
-- `omFromPairs` inserts left to right and a later insert wins).  Measured on the
-- corpus rather than reasoned from the source: the golden's `iface:Echo 1 mod:beta`
-- spread line is that rule's output, `beta` being the SECOND import below.
--
-- That choice is PINNED rather than endorsed.  A silent winner is the input the
-- `AmbiguousInterface` diagnostic (unit E/F of Stage A-1) has to REPLACE; the
-- honest end state here is a diagnostic, and when it lands this line moves.
--
-- ⚠️ This file compiles and runs at exit 0 today, printing `alpha-cat`, with no
-- diagnostic of any kind. That is not a property to preserve — it is #1047 on the
-- page.  PR C changes none of it; it only makes the identities observable.

import alpha.{Speak, Echo, Cat(..), speak, twice, echo}
import beta.{Speak, Echo, Dog(..), Kennel(..)}

data Pair a = Pair a a

-- A `=>` predicate naming `Speak` at a use site in a THIRD module — neither
-- declarer's own.
sayIt : Speak a => a -> String
sayIt x = speak x

-- An `impl` with a `requires`, both naming `Speak`, in that same third module: the
-- `DImpl.implOrigin` and `Require.requireOrigin` carriers side by side.
impl Speak (Pair a) requires Speak a where
  speak (Pair x _) = speak x

-- THE PRECEDENCE WITNESS (see alpha.mdk).  `Echo` is declared by both alpha and
-- beta and used by neither, so this `=>` is its ONLY occurrence anywhere in the
-- corpus and `HEAD SPREAD`'s `iface:Echo 1 mod:<winner>` line is exactly the
-- declaration this import pair resolved to.
resound : Echo a => a -> String
resound x = echo x

main = println "\{sayIt (Pair Cat Cat)} \{twice Cat} \{speak Dog} \{speak (Kennel Dog)}"
